  "helpStartSectionTitle": "Getting Started",
  "helpStartOnboardingTitle": "Setting up ShiftBell",
  "helpStartOnboardingBody": "Allow notifications, exact alarms and display over other apps so ShiftBell can alert you reliably. Then choose whether your shifts repeat, add your shift names and set alarms for the shifts that need them. You can skip alarms and add them later in Settings.\n\nRemove unused shift names with × and add any missing names with + Add. If you have used ShiftBell before, choose Restore from backup to restore a backup file.\n\nOpen Settings → Alarm permissions in ShiftBell to check each permission, including lock screen display. Return to the app after changing settings.",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesTitle": "Repeating vs. irregular schedules",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesBody": "A repeating schedule follows a cycle, such as Day, Day, Night, Night, Off, Off. Build the cycle and select today’s position in it. ShiftBell fills in future dates automatically.\n\nFor a non-repeating schedule, assign shifts to dates yourself. Long-press a date, select any other dates you want, and choose a shift.",
  "helpShiftSectionTitle": "Managing Shifts",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignTitle": "Adding shifts without a repeating pattern",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignBody": "Touch and hold a date on the calendar, select any other dates you need, then choose a shift. The shift is assigned only to the dates you select.\n\nAdd upcoming shifts in advance so ShiftBell can schedule their alarms.",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftTitle": "Changing the shift on a specific date",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftBody": "Whether you're on a repeating or irregular schedule, you can override a single date by long-pressing it on the calendar and picking a different shift. Alarms for that day are automatically recalculated to match the new shift.",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsTitle": "Customizing shift colors",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsBody": "Every calendar theme has default colors per shift, but you can override the color of a specific shift name if you like. Pick the shift name in the shift settings and choose a color — your choice takes priority over the theme's default.",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsTitle": "Viewing other teams in your rotation",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsBody": "Tap All teams on the Calendar tab to compare team schedules. Add team names and choose your team. You can use one repeating pattern with a different starting position for each team, or set a separate cycle or weekly schedule for each team.\n\nTeam schedules are calculated on your device. They do not connect to coworkers’ accounts or download their schedules.",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeTitle": "When your team changes",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeBody": "If you have set up team schedules, open All teams → Edit → Change my team and select your new team. Shift alarms will follow that team’s schedule. Manual changes to individual dates will be cleared, but the team schedules will be kept.\n\nTo change your position in a repeating pattern instead, go to Settings → Edit → Change Schedule. Check the confirmation message before saving.",
  "helpAlarmSectionTitle": "Alarms",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesTitle": "Setting alarms for a shift",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesBody": "Add up to five alarms per shift in Settings. Each alarm has its own time and sound settings and can ring on the shift day, the day before or the day after.\n\nShiftBell prepares upcoming alarms automatically as your calendar moves forward. For a non-repeating schedule, assign your shifts ahead of time so their alarms can be scheduled.\n\nAlarm lists use a 24-hour display. The time editor follows your device’s 12- or 24-hour setting. In 12-hour mode, 12 AM is midnight and 12 PM is noon.\n\nShift alarms follow the device’s current time zone. When daylight saving time skips a clock time, the alarm moves forward by the clock change (for example, 02:30 becomes 03:30 for a one-hour jump). When a clock time occurs twice, it rings once at the second occurrence. Alarms that resolve to the same time are combined.",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeTitle": "Choosing an alarm sound and volume",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeBody": "You can choose from 7 built-in alarm sounds or your device's default alarm sound. Speaker characteristics vary by device, so the app auto-corrects volume, but if it still feels off, try switching to a different sound in the alarm settings.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingTitle": "If an alarm doesn't go off",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingBody": "Check that exact alarms are allowed for ShiftBell. Also check notification and display-over-other-apps permissions so you can see and control alarms, and review your phone’s battery restrictions.\n\nMake sure the date has a shift assigned and that the shift has an alarm. Check the alarm’s sound, volume and vibration settings as well.\n\nOpen Settings → Alarm permissions in ShiftBell to check each permission, including lock screen display. Return to the app after changing settings.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryTitle": "Viewing alarm history",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryBody": "You can see every alarm and its result under Settings → Alarm History.\n\nSnooze schedules the next ring after the number of minutes shown on the alarm screen. Adjust it with −/+; change the default in Settings → Sound & Vibration. The selected interval is preserved when the clocks change. During the repeated hour when clocks go back, “First occurrence” and “Second occurrence” distinguish the two times in upcoming alarms and alarm history.\n\nIf another alarm is already set for the snooze time, the current alarm is dismissed without snoozing. The other alarm is kept.",
  "helpCalendarSectionTitle": "Calendar & Home Widget",
  "helpCalendarThemesTitle": "Changing the calendar theme",
  "helpCalendarThemesBody": "Choose a calendar theme in Settings → Calendar Theme.\n\nThemes apply to the calendar inside the app. The home-screen widget uses its own colors.",
  "helpCalendarOtTitle": "Recording and checking overtime",
  "helpCalendarOtBody": "Tap a date in the Calendar tab to record overtime. The app includes it in your weekly and monthly work-hour totals. OT means overtime.\n\nWeekly totals always run from Monday to Sunday, even when your calendar starts on Sunday. Monthly totals use the reporting period you choose in Settings. These are time totals, not a payroll calculation.",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayTitle": "Checking weekly and monthly work hours",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayBody": "Enter the usual start and end times for each shift in Settings. ShiftBell uses these times and your overtime entries to calculate weekly and monthly hours.\n\nFor a repeating pattern, you can also include extra hours from shift changes in the overtime summary. Only the increase over the original shift counts, not the new shift’s full duration.",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetTitle": "Adding the home-screen widget",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetBody": "Long-press your home screen and add 3-week calendar or 1-week calendar from Widgets. The 3-week calendar shows the year and month, followed by last week, this week and next week. The 1-week calendar shows weekday labels and the current week. Both show shift names and up to three notes per date. You can resize the width without changing the number of weeks.\n\nLong shift names shrink to fit without being shortened. Weekday order and red dates follow the app’s regional rules. Backgrounds and day/night indicators use your device’s local time, not weather data.",
  "helpBackupSectionTitle": "Backup & Restore",
  "helpBackupDataStorageTitle": "Where is my data stored?",
  "helpBackupDataStorageBody": "Your shifts, alarms, alarm history, notes and work-hour records are saved on your device. Backups are local files in Download/ShiftBell. They are not uploaded automatically.\n\nUsage statistics and advertising use Google services. See the Privacy Policy for what information they process.\n\nSleep records are also stored on your device and included in local backups.",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupTitle": "When does automatic backup happen?",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupBody": "ShiftBell checks for changes when you open, return to or leave the app. If your data has changed, it saves an automatic backup to Download/ShiftBell on your device. You can also save a backup using Settings → Additional features → Data backup → Save backup.\n\nIt keeps one manual backup and one automatic backup. A new backup replaces only the file of the same kind. Backups require Android 10 or later.",
  "helpBackupRestoreTitle": "Restoring after reinstalling or moving to a new device",
  "helpBackupRestoreBody": "On the first screen, choose Restore from backup. You can also use Settings → Additional features → Data backup → Restore from backup. Select a file from Download/ShiftBell and review its saved time: restoring replaces your schedule, notes, overtime and other saved data and settings.\n\nAlarm history and creation records are merged with the backup. Alarms that are ringing or snoozed are kept.\n\nTo move to another phone, copy the file to that phone first. After restoring, check your shifts, upcoming alarms and alarm permissions.\n\nSleep records are also included in the restore.",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualTitle": "If automatic restore doesn't find your backup",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualBody": "After reinstalling, choose Restore from backup and select your previous backup using the file picker. Android may not let the new installation find files created by the old installation automatically.",
  "helpTroubleshootSectionTitle": "Troubleshooting",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueTitle": "My alarm didn't go off",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueBody": "1. Check permissions and phone settings\nOpen Settings → Alarm permissions and check notifications, exact alarms, lock screen display and display over other apps.\n\nAlso check Phone settings → Apps → ShiftBell → Battery for restrictions on background use.\n\n2. Check shifts and shift alarms\nCheck that a shift is assigned to the date in the calendar. In Settings → Edit → Edit Shift Alarms, check that an alarm is set for that shift. Assigning a shift alone does not create an alarm if no shift alarm has been set.\n\n3. Alarm operation by manufacturer\nOn the Samsung, Motorola, OPPO, Xiaomi, Google Pixel and realme devices we checked, we confirmed normal alarm operation with the required permissions configured.\n\nOn devices from other manufacturers, alarms may fail to ring or may not run at the scheduled time even when permissions are allowed. We are expanding manufacturer verification and continuing to improve alarm reliability.\n\n4. If the problem continues\nContact the developer at lowvibe07@gmail.com with your device model, Android version and the time of the problem. You can also attach a log using Settings → Additional features → Email diagnostic log to help identify the cause.",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopTitle": "I force-stopped this app from phone settings",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopBody": "Using Force stop in Android Settings → Apps cancels ShiftBell’s scheduled alarms. Open ShiftBell again before your next alarm so it can reschedule upcoming alarms. Alarms whose time has already passed will not ring retroactively.\n\nLeaving the app or removing it from Recent apps does not itself cancel alarms, although some phones apply additional background restrictions.",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueTitle": "The widget isn't updating",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueBody": "Try removing the widget and adding it again. If it still looks the same, open the app once, then go back to your home screen and check again.",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingTitle": "I can't find my backup file",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingBody": "Look in Download/ShiftBell using your phone’s file manager. If no backup is there, create one using Settings → Additional features → Data backup → Save backup.",
  "startupFailedTitle": "Couldn't start the app",
  "startupFailedBody": "Something went wrong while getting the app ready. Make sure your device has enough free storage, then try again. If this keeps happening, try restarting your device.",
  "startupRetry": "Try again",
  "startupSlowMessage": "Getting the app ready. Please wait a moment.",
  "startupStalledMessage": "This is taking longer than usual. If you still see this screen after trying again, close the app completely and open it again.",
  "onboardingBackupTitle": "Restore from backup",
  "onboardingBackupDesc": "Start from a backup file in Download/ShiftBell.",
  "helpCalendarTextSizeTitle": "Calendar text and screen sizes",
  "helpCalendarTextSizeBody": "Calendar cells adapt to phone, cover and unfolded screen widths. Long shift names and notes may be shortened to fit. Tap a date or note to read the full text. Each date supports up to three notes.\n\nSimple Line and Soft Card use a larger default shift label. Calendar text scaling is limited to keep dates, shifts and notes within their cells.",
  "helpDiagnosticTitle": "Send a diagnostic log",
  "helpDiagnosticBody": "Use Settings → Additional features → Email diagnostic log to send a .txt file to the developer. Choose your sending account in your email app and complete sending there. The recipient is lowvibe07@gmail.com.\n\nOn Android 10 or later, you can also find automatically saved diagnostic files in Download/ShiftBell/log using your file manager.\n\nThey include device and Android versions, permission and battery settings, and alarm and backup processing records. Note contents and shift names are excluded. Files are not sent automatically. You can attach them when contacting support. They may remain after uninstalling; remove them in your file manager when no longer needed.",
  "helpCalendarLocaleTitle": "Dates and week start",
  "helpCalendarLocaleBody": "The calendar uses Sunday first for US English and Monday first for UK English and supported European regional settings. English dates use month names to avoid confusion between day/month and month/day.\n\nA red Sunday marks the weekend. Your shifts and alarms follow your own schedule.\n\nWeekly work-hour totals always use Monday–Sunday. A weekend does not automatically make a shift a day off.",
