  "helpConditionSectionTitle": "Sleep & recovery",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItTitle": "What does Sleep & recovery show?",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItBody": "This optional tab combines your shifts with confirmed sleep records. It shows facts such as rest between shifts, sleep after work and consecutive night shifts, along with suggestions on sleep, caffeine and the journey home. It does not give a health score or diagnosis. The work-load level is based on your work schedule; sleep records inform the sleep guidance. Missing records or shift times are explained separately. The guidance draws on sleep and occupational-health research.",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListTitle": "Sources behind the guidance",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededTitle": "Setting up shift-based guidance",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededBody": "In Settings → Work Hours & Overtime, enter start and end times for at least one shift you use. You do not need to fill in every shift. Without shift times, you can still record sleep and see available sleep summaries; shift-based recovery guidance needs those times.",
  "helpSleepSectionTitle": "Sleep records",
  "helpSleepWidgetTitle": "Recording with the sleep widget",
  "helpSleepWidgetBody": "Add the sleep widget to your home screen. Tap “Sleep” when going to bed and “Wake up” when you get up. It also shows your shift for today. Records appear in Sleep & recovery alongside your shifts. Enable the feature in Settings first; when it is off, widget recording and automatic estimates are disabled.",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectTitle": "How automatic sleep estimates work",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectBody": "When enabled, the app periodically checks whether the screen is off and whether the device is charging during sleep windows derived from your shifts. It does not continuously monitor phone use and cannot prove you were asleep. Automatic candidates are shown only after at least 5 hours. Log short naps or sleep during work manually or with the widget.\n\nEstimates count in sleep summaries only after you confirm them. You can edit or discard them. Turning off Sleep & recovery stops future estimation.",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarTitle": "Viewing and editing sleep records",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarBody": "The mini calendar shows confirmed sleep by its assigned sleep day. Tap a slot to add, edit or delete a record. “View all” lets you browse earlier months. The sleep pattern card compares main sleep and all recorded naps over the past 30 days, after at least 3 recorded days per shift type. Days without records are excluded.\n\nSleep after a night shift can belong to the day that shift started. Main sleep is not simply any sleep lasting 2 hours: sleep overlapping work or preparing for a night shift may be a nap; among eligible records, the longest is main sleep.\n\nTimes use the device’s current time zone. During a clock change, an unavailable time is rejected and a repeated time requires choosing the first or second occurrence. Duration uses actual elapsed time.",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListBody": "The guidance refers to the 14 sources below. Some cutoffs, such as general consecutive-day warnings and comparisons with your own average, are app rules. Research findings describe groups, not your individual risk. EU legal references and nursing recommendations are comparisons, not rules that necessarily apply in your country.\n\nEVIDENCE-001 · Rotation direction — Forward rotation (day → evening → night) may be easier to adjust to than backward rotation. NIOSH training; no single schedule is best for everyone.\n\nEVIDENCE-002 · Rest between shifts — A minimum of 11 hours between shifts is recommended. Knauth & Hornberger, cited by NIOSH Module 5.\n\nEVIDENCE-003 · Daily rest — Article 3 of EU Directive 2003/88/EC sets an 11-hour consecutive daily-rest reference. Its scope, exceptions and national implementation matter.\n\nEVIDENCE-004 · Long shifts — A research model estimated relative injury risk about 13% higher for 10-hour shifts and 27% higher for 12-hour shifts than for 8-hour shifts. Folkard & Lombardi, 2006. These are not personal risk estimates.\n\nEVIDENCE-005 · Rest days — Consider 1–2 full days off after five 8-hour or four 10-hour shifts, and 2 days after three 12-hour shifts. NIOSH also cautions against long work stretches followed by compressed breaks.\n\nEVIDENCE-006 · Sleep after night work — Prioritise sleep after a night shift. NIOSH sleep guidance.\n\nEVIDENCE-007 · Pre-shift naps — The AASM May 2025 public-comment guideline version conditionally suggests pre-night-shift naps for adults with shift work disorder and excessive sleepiness; certainty is very low. Allow time for grogginess to pass before driving.\n\nEVIDENCE-008 · Caffeine — In a trial, 400 mg taken even 6 hours before bedtime reduced sleep by more than an hour. Drake et al., 2013. Effects vary with dose and person.\n\nEVIDENCE-009 · Light on the journey home — Reducing bright light may help subsequent sleep. Drowsy-driving safety takes priority. NIOSH.\n\nEVIDENCE-010 · Food during night work — NIOSH suggests smaller, lighter meals between midnight and 06:00 and avoiding heavy meals shortly before sleep.\n\nEVIDENCE-011 · Adult sleep duration — The National Sleep Foundation recommends 7–9 hours for adults aged 18–64 (2015 expert consensus). Individual needs vary.\n\nEVIDENCE-012 · Weekly work — Article 6 of EU Directive 2003/88/EC uses an average 48-hour weekly limit including overtime over the applicable reference period. Exceptions and national rules can apply.\n\nEVIDENCE-013 · Consecutive nights — A research model reported cumulative relative injury risk, compared with night one: about +6% on night two, +17% on night three and +36% on night four. Folkard & Tucker, 2003. The app does not calculate personal accident probabilities.\n\nEVIDENCE-014 · Nursing hours — IOM (2004) recommended no more than 12 hours in 24 and 60 hours in 7 days for nurses. A 2014 systematic review associated weeks above 40 hours with poorer patient safety. Both are cited in NIOSH Module 5; they are not universal legal limits.",
